## Who decides what

{{principal_name}} — {{principal_title}} — decides:
{{#principal_decides}}- {{.}}
{{/principal_decides}}

Everything else is delegated to the persona that owns it. The delegation is real:
a lead who has to ask before every call is not a lead, and an org that routes
every decision to the top decides nothing well.

## The line, and the two deliberate exceptions

Most personas sit in a delivery chain and report to the role accountable for
their output.

{{#staff}}
**{{name}} — {{title}} — is staff.** Outside every delivery chain, reporting to
{{principal_name}} directly. Owns no delivery work and assigns none.
{{/staff}}

Staff roles exist because some functions stop working the moment they report
into what they assess. A reviewer who reports to the person being reviewed
becomes a rubber stamp — not through bad faith, but because the incentive is
structural and patience is finite.

The same logic applies wherever a role audits another: put it beside, not
beneath.

